 ## Overview
-This method converts a set of 2D pixel coordinates into a 3D triangle mesh representing a voxelized image, where each pixel corresponds to a 1mm cube [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of generating efficient surface geometry from discrete 2D data by culling interior faces between adjacent same-group pixels to minimize triangle count [S1, unofficial]. It differs from general surface extraction methods like Marching Cubes by operating directly on grid-aligned pixel data without interpolating scalar fields, and from blocky meshing methods that process 3D voxel volumes by treating the input as a 2D heightmap-like structure extruded into Z [S1, unofficial].
+This method converts a set of 2D pixel coordinates into a 3D triangle mesh representing a voxelized image, where each pixel corresponds to a unit cube [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of generating efficient surface geometry from discrete 2D data by emitting only exterior faces and culling interior faces between adjacent same-group pixels [S1, unofficial]. Unlike general surface extraction methods that operate on 3D volumes, this approach operates directly on 2D pixel grids, assuming a fixed depth (Z-axis) extent [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Voxel Block Placement with Face Culling](923-voxel-block-placement-with-face-culling.md) by treating the input as a flat set of pixels rather than a 3D block structure, and from [Face Coverage Mask Culling](3397-face-coverage-mask-culling.md) by using simple cardinal neighbor checks rather than projected coverage masks.
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-*   `pixels`: A list of 2D integer coordinates `(X, Y)` representing occupied pixel positions.
-*   `imageHeight`: An integer representing the height of the source image grid, used for the final rotation transformation.
